q:
An IAM user has the following two policies attached:
Policy 1: {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": "s3:*", "Resource": "*"}
Policy 2: {"Effect": "Deny", "Action": "s3:*", "Resource": "*", "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "false"}}}
The user makes an API call using access keys WITHOUT MFA. What happens?

A trust policy has two separate Condition blocks:
"Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "12345"}}
and in a separate statement:
"Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalOrgID": "o-abc123"}}
How is this evaluated compared to having both in one block?

Two IAM policies are attached to a user:
Policy 1: {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": "ec2:*", "Resource": "*", "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"ec2:ResourceTag/Environment": "Production"}}}
Policy 2: {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": "ec2:*", "Resource": "*", "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"ec2:ResourceTag/Environment": "Development"}}}
Can the user perform EC2 actions on a Staging instance?

A company implements the following policy for developers:
{"Effect": "Allow", "Action": "iam:CreateRole", "Resource": "*", "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"iam:PermissionsBoundary": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:policy/DevBoundary"}}}
A developer creates a role WITHOUT specifying a permissions boundary. What happens?
